---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'FMLA Violation Damages Calculator | fmlaviolationcalc.com';
const description = 'Estimate potential damages for Family and Medical Leave Act (FMLA) violations: back pay, liquidated damages, front pay, and reinstatement. Free, informational.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>FMLA Violation Damages Calculator</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <p>The Family and Medical Leave Act (FMLA) entitles eligible employees at covered employers to up to 12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected leave per year for qualifying family and medical reasons. Employers who interfere with FMLA rights or retaliate against employees who exercise them are liable for back pay, liquidated damages, and attorney fees. This calculator estimates potential FMLA recovery based on your situation — it is an educational tool, not legal advice.</p>
  <h2>Run the estimate</h2>
  <form id="calc" novalidate>
    <label for="salary">Annual salary (USD)</label>
    <input id="salary" type="number" value="65000" min="0" />
    <label for="violation">Type of FMLA violation</label>
    <select id="violation">
      <option value="termination">Terminated for taking or requesting FMLA leave</option>
      <option value="interference">Employer interfered with or denied FMLA leave</option>
      <option value="retaliation">Retaliation after returning from FMLA leave</option>
      <option value="demotion">Demotion or pay cut upon return</option>
    </select>
    <label for="months">Months of lost employment (if terminated or constructively discharged)</label>
    <input id="months" type="number" value="6" min="0" max="60" />
    <label for="good-faith">Did employer act in bad faith (knowing violation)?</label>
    <select id="good-faith">
      <option value="no">No — employer claims good-faith mistake</option>
      <option value="yes">Yes — employer knowingly violated FMLA or showed reckless disregard</option>
    </select>
    <label for="found-work">Have you found comparable replacement work?</label>
    <select id="found-work">
      <option value="no">Not yet</option>
      <option value="partial">Partial replacement income</option>
      <option value="yes">Yes — comparable position found</option>
    </select>
    <button type="submit">Estimate damages</button>
  </form>
  <p id="result" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <h2>How FMLA damages work</h2>
  <p>The FMLA provides for: (1) lost wages and benefits (back pay) from the date of the violation through judgment; (2) an equal amount in liquidated damages unless the employer proves good faith and reasonable grounds for the violation; (3) front pay when reinstatement is not feasible; and (4) attorney fees and costs. Liquidated damages &mdash; the doubling of back pay &mdash; are the most significant element for most claims.</p>
  <p>Reinstatement to the same or equivalent position is also available. When reinstatement is impractical (the position was eliminated, the relationship is too damaged, or the employee prefers front pay), courts award front pay instead. The FMLA&rsquo;s two-year statute of limitations (three years for willful violations) runs from the date of the violation, making prompt action important for preserving full recovery.</p>
  <h2>Understanding your estimate</h2>
  <p>The calculator applies the statutory damage framework from 29 U.S.C. &sect; 2617: back pay adjusted for mitigation, a liquidated damages component based on whether bad faith is found, and a front pay estimate for termination cases without replacement employment. The range output (60%&ndash;140% of the base estimate) reflects the inherent uncertainty in FMLA damages, which depend heavily on how quickly the employee finds new work, how a jury assesses bad faith, and how a judge calculates front pay. For interference and demotion cases without termination, back pay is capped at 25% of annual salary reflecting actual wage losses rather than full employment loss. Consult an employment attorney for a case-specific evaluation.</p>
  <h2>Where to learn more</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-fmla-damages-work">how FMLA damages work</a>, <a href="/guides/types-of-fmla-violations">types of FMLA violations</a>, <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-fmla-violation">what to do after an FMLA violation</a>, and <a href="/guides/fmla-misconceptions">common FMLA misconceptions</a>. The <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> covers eligibility, intermittent leave, and filing deadlines.</p>
  <script>
    document.getElementById('calc').addEventListener('submit', e => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const salary = parseFloat(document.getElementById('salary').value)||65000;
      const months = parseFloat(document.getElementById('months').value)||0;
      const violation = document.getElementById('violation').value;
      const badFaith = document.getElementById('good-faith').value==='yes';
      const foundWork = document.getElementById('found-work').value;
      const mitigation = foundWork==='yes' ? 0.7 : (foundWork==='partial' ? 0.35 : 0);
      const backPay = (salary/12)*months*(1-mitigation);
      const liquidated = badFaith ? backPay : backPay * 0.5; // 50% chance of liquidated even without clear bad faith
      const frontPay = (violation==='termination'||violation==='retaliation') && foundWork!=='yes' ? salary * 0.5 : 0;
      // For non-termination violations, damages are limited to actual losses
      const adjBack = (violation==='interference' || violation==='demotion') ? Math.min(backPay, salary * 0.25) : backPay;
      const total = adjBack + liquidated + frontPay;
      const fmt = n=>'$'+Math.round(n).toLocaleString();
      document.getElementById('result').innerHTML =
        `<strong>Estimated range: ${fmt(total*0.6)} &ndash; ${fmt(total*1.4)}</strong><br>Back pay: ${fmt(adjBack)} &bull; Liquidated damages: ${fmt(liquidated)} &bull; Front pay: ${fmt(frontPay)}<br><em>Attorney fees recoverable separately under 29 U.S.C. § 2617. Reinstatement may also be available. Consult an employment attorney.</em>`;
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
